<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-laplace-table-linearity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Laplace Transform Table and Linearity</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NAMED FORMULA FACTS (xref targets for hover preview)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->



  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       LAPLACE TRANSFORM TABLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the previous section we computed two Laplace transforms directly from the definition.
    From now on, we will use the following table to look up transforms directly.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-laplace-transforms">
    <title>Laplace Transform Table</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}</m></cell>
        <cell>Condition</cell>
        <cell>Formula</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>A</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>B</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>C</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>D</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>E</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>H</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\sin(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{b}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>I</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\cos(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>J</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       LINEARITY MOTIVATION: INTEGRATION AND DIFFERENTIATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Before we use the table, let's talk about <em>linearity</em>. You already know two linear
    operations from calculus: differentiation and integration. For example:
    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{d}{dt}\bigl[3t^2 + 5\cos(t)\bigr]
        \amp= 3 \cdot \frac{d}{dt}[t^2] + 5 \cdot \frac{d}{dt}[\cos(t)]</mrow>
      <mrow>\int \bigl[3t^2 + 5\cos(t)\bigr]\,dt
        \amp= 3\int t^2\,dt + 5\int \cos(t)\,dt.</mrow>
    </md>
    In both cases you can split over a sum and pull constants out front. The Laplace transform
    behaves the same way, because it is defined by an integral.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: LINEARITY OF THE LAPLACE TRANSFORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-laplace-linearity">
    <title>Linearity of the Laplace Transform</title>
    <p>
      The Laplace transform is linear. That is, for any functions <m>f(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m>
      and constants <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,
      <me>\mathcal{L}\{af(t) + bg(t)\} = a\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} + b\mathcal{L}\{g(t)\}.</me>
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       JUSTIFICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-laplace-linearity-justification">
    <title>Justification (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      Using the definition directly:
      <md>
        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{f + g\}
          \amp= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st}(f+g)\,dt
           = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st}f\,dt + \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st}g\,dt
           = \mathcal{L}\{f\} + \mathcal{L}\{g\}</mrow>
        <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{cf\}
          \amp= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st}(cf)\,dt
           = c\int_0^{\infty} e^{-st}f\,dt
           = c\,\mathcal{L}\{f\}.</mrow>
      </md>
      Both steps are just the linearity of integration.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <p>
    In the examples below we will show each linearity step explicitly so you can see
    the pattern. Going forward, you may start skipping those steps automatically,
    just like you do with differentiation and integration!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: L{t^6 - 4e^{8t}}, FULL WORK SHOWN INLINE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-laplace-table-basic">
    <title>Using the Table and Linearity</title>
    <p>
      Compute <m>\mathcal{L}\{t^6 - 4e^{8t}\}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apply linearity to split over the sum and pull the constant out front:
      <me>\mathcal{L}\{t^6 - 4e^{8t}\} = \mathcal{L}\{t^6\} - 4\,\mathcal{L}\{e^{8t}\}.</me>
      Now apply the table to each piece. For <m>t^6</m>, use <xref ref="formula-C"/> with <m>n = 6</m>:
      <me>\mathcal{L}\{t^6\} = \frac{6!}{s^{6+1}} = \frac{6!}{s^7}.</me>
      For <m>e^{8t}</m>, use <xref ref="formula-B"/> with <m>a = 8</m>:
      <me>\mathcal{L}\{e^{8t}\} = \frac{1}{s-8}.</me>
      Putting it together:
      <me>\mathcal{L}\{t^6 - 4e^{8t}\} = \frac{6!}{s^7} - \frac{4}{s-8}.</me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: L{(e^{2t} + 3)sin(9t) + cos(9t)}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-laplace-linearity-practice">
    <title>Compute <m>\mathcal{L}\{(e^{2t} + 3)\sin(9t) + \cos(9t)\}</m></title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Work through the following steps to compute the Laplace transform.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-laplace-linearity-mc">
      <title>Step 1: Apply Linearity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          First distribute and then apply linearity to split the expression into separate
          Laplace transforms. Which of the following is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t)\} + 3\,\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\} + \mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Distributing gives <m>e^{2t}\sin(9t) + 3\sin(9t) + \cos(9t)</m>,
              and then linearity splits this into three separate transforms with the 3 pulled out front.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\}\cdot\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\} + 3\,\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\} + \mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The Laplace transform of a product is not the product of the transforms.
              The term <m>e^{2t}\sin(9t)</m> is a single piece that matches a row in the table directly.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t) + 3\sin(9t)\} + \mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is technically true by linearity, but the split is incomplete.
              Separate every term so you can apply the table to each one individually.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t)\} + \mathcal{L}\{3\sin(9t) + \cos(9t)\}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Same issue: the split is incomplete. Keep going until each term is alone
              so you can match each one to a row in the table.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-laplace-linearity-compute">
      <title>Step 2: Apply the Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now evaluate each of the three Laplace transforms using the table and simplify fully.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For each term, identify which formula it matches and what values play the role
          of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t)\}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-I"/> with <m>a = 2</m>, <m>b = 9</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-D"/> with <m>a = 9</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-E"/> with <m>a = 9</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <title>Work (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          More details on how to apply the table to each term are included in a dropdown at the end of this solution.
          Starting from the result of Step 1 and substituting each table value:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}\{(e^{2t}+3)\sin(9t)+\cos(9t)\}
              \amp= \mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t)\} + 3\,\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\} + \mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{9}{(s-2)^2+81} + 3 \cdot \frac{9}{s^2+81} + \frac{s}{s^2+81}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{9}{(s-2)^2+81} + \frac{27+s}{s^2+81}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The last two terms share the denominator <m>s^2 + 81</m>, so their numerators combine.
        </p>

        <p>You can reference the following work if you are unsure on how we applied the table in the second line! </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Identifying a and b for each term and applying the table</title>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li><p>
                <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{2t}\sin(9t)\}</m>: matches <xref ref="formula-I" text="custom">Formula I</xref>
                with <m>a = 2</m> and <m>b = 9</m>, giving
                <m>\dfrac{9}{(s-2)^2 + 9^2} = \dfrac{9}{(s-2)^2+81}</m>.
              </p></li>
              <li><p>
                <m>3\,\mathcal{L}\{\sin(9t)\}</m>: matches <xref ref="formula-D" text="custom">Formula D</xref>
                with <m>a = 9</m>, giving
                <m>3 \cdot \dfrac{9}{s^2 + 9^2} = \dfrac{27}{s^2+81}</m>.
              </p></li>
              <li><p>
                <m>\mathcal{L}\{\cos(9t)\}</m>: matches <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref>
                with <m>a = 9</m>, giving
                <m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + 9^2} = \dfrac{s}{s^2+81}</m>.
              </p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The table:</alert> you can look up Laplace transforms directly without
          computing from the definition.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Linearity:</alert> <m>\mathcal{L}\{af + bg\} = a\mathcal{L}\{f\} + b\mathcal{L}\{g\}</m>,
          just like differentiation and integration.
          This works because the Laplace transform is defined by an integral.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Strategy:</alert> distribute and expand first, then split by linearity,
          then match each piece to a row in the table.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

